module v2.workflow.floor_terminal_ledger

// THE FLOOR'S TERMINAL EVIDENCE, OWNED BY v2, TO BE SERIALIZED BY THE SEED.
//
// The v2 self-host program claims candidate-bound witnesses executed and matched their
// expectations. The seed is the only boundary holding that per-identity fact and it discards the
// passing half before v2 can read it: the accumulation branch increments a counter and drops the
// identity, so a run that reports `passed=10132` cannot say WHICH 10132. This module is the
// schema and the completeness law for the evidence the floor already observes. It invents no
// verdict and reclassifies none.
//
// A PASS LIST IS THE WRONG ARTIFACT AND THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT ONE. A roster of passing
// identities reproduces one layer up exactly the ambiguity it was built to remove: an identity
// ABSENT from a pass list is failed, interrupted, unrouted, never executed, or lost to truncation
// -- five states behind one silence, which is the state-space conflation this repository already
// names as a recurring failure. The ledger therefore carries ONE ROW PER PLANNED IDENTITY and
// every row is terminal. Absence is not a value it can express.
//
// THE SEED CARRIES A HAND-WRITTEN MIRROR OF THIS SCHEMA. `cli_run.rs` is HAND_MAINTAINED, so its
// `ClaimTerminalRow`, `ClaimDisposition` and `reconcile_terminal_ledger` are authored beside these
// declarations rather than derived from them. Regen does not compare them, because that file is
// not generated; the witnesses do not compare them, because each side is exercised alone.
//
// THAT MIRROR IS TWO ERROR CLASSES AT TWO DIFFERENT RUNGS, AND ONE ROW OVER BOTH IS WRONG IN BOTH
// DIRECTIONS -- it under-rates what the comparator actually guarantees and over-rates what the
// declarations do. An earlier revision of this note carried the single row, and the correction is
// recorded rather than silently applied because "the mirror is mitigatable" was cited as settled.
//
// CLASS 1 -- `TerminalDispositionWireAgreement`.  Rung: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE.
//   Scope:     every terminal row admitted by this artifact path.
//   Guarantee: the disposition the seed derived equals the disposition obtained by decoding the
//              tag it emitted. A disagreement refuses the whole ledger and names the identity.
//   Not structural: an invalid row is still CONSTRUCTIBLE -- the seed can compute a wrong pair --
//              it simply cannot become accepted evidence. Construction would require the two
//              derivations to be one, which is the dissolution below, not a check.
//   NON-CLAIM, and it is the load-bearing half: neither derivation is proven semantically correct
//              in isolation. Two mappings that are JOINTLY wrong in the same direction agree, and
//              agreement is all this class checks. It catches DIVERGENCE, never error.
//   Coverage bound: the join runs over every row of every run, which is the OBSERVED population
//              and not every mapping ARM. An arm no live row reaches is unexercised and the join
//              is silent about it -- §4b(1) rung honesty, since a class's rung is the minimum over
//              its in-scope paths and an unexercised arm is not on any of them.
//
// CLASS 2 -- `SeedDagTerminalSchemaDeclarationLockstep`.  Rung: MITIGATABLE.
//   Scope:     the declaration vocabulary itself -- payload shapes, arm existence, field contracts.
//   Held by:   author diligence. Nothing executes over it.
//   The production join above does NOT retire this, and these all still pass unnoticed:
//     - Rust grows a variant no live row produces
//     - this module grows an arm no run exercises
//     - both sides keep a tag and change its MEANING together
//     - a payload or cardinality diverges under an unchanged tag
//     - both mappings are jointly wrong in the same direction (Class 1's stated non-claim)
//     - a variant disappears from one side but the population never reaches it
//   Next-rung trigger: a calibration population carrying exactly one row per terminal disposition,
//              mutation-tested in both directions independently. That converts "enforced for what
//              ran" into "enforced for every declared arm". Its discriminating case is a mutation
//              that must NOT red -- change a payload contract while keeping every tag, and Class 1
//              stays green while this class is the only thing that could have caught it. Without
//              that case the two rows are indistinguishable and this one is decoration.
//
// DISSOLUTION -- one event retires the mirror and the seam together: the self-emitted v2 claim
// executor. Once v2 drives the floor's execution, the producer holding identity-and-outcome
// together is v2 itself, the seed's copy is deleted whole, and this module is the sole authority
// it already claims to be. Until then the duplication is admitted debt with a named terminal,
// not an unremarked second representation.

// WHAT A CLAIM'S ATTEMPT ACTUALLY REACHED, at the grain the executor already distinguishes.
//
// These arms are not a taxonomy invented here: they mirror the arms the seed ALREADY holds
// typed, one for one. `ClaimOutcome` in the seed is Pass | Fail | NotBool { got } | RuntimeError
// { message } | TimedOut { elapsed_ms, budget_ms, kind, completion } | HostToolUnresolved { name,
// probed } | (route gap), and its own comment at that match says why: "Every non-pass arm is
// reported with the fact that distinguishes it. A collapsed `failed` would make a budget refusal,
// a runtime error and a witness that answered false read alike, and those three have different
// remedies."
//
// AN EARLIER DRAFT COLLAPSED ALL OF THEM INTO `DiagnosedStop { reason: v2.std.node.Symbol }`, which is the
// same defect the seed's comment argues against, committed on the way ACROSS the seam rather than
// at the match. A `v2.std.node.Symbol` reason is not the raw terminal fact -- it is a rendering of one, and
// the boundary this schema is built for is "v1 preserves the raw terminal fact it already holds".
// Stringing it during transport would have made the ledger strictly less informative than the
// producer, which is the opposite of the obligation. `BudgetRefused` carries the existing
// `v2.workflow.required_floor.ClaimSafetyOutcome` rather than a second spelling of it.
type ClaimAttemptTerminal
  = Returned { observation: ClaimObservation }
  | RuntimeErrored { message: v2.std.text.String }
  | BudgetRefused { safety: v2.workflow.required_floor.ClaimSafetyOutcome }
  | HostToolUnresolved { name: v2.std.text.String, probed: List<v2.std.text.String> }
  | RouteGap { route: v2.std.text.String }

// AN ATTEMPT THAT RETURNED STILL MAY NOT HAVE OBSERVED ANYTHING. A claim whose result is not a
// verdict the floor can read -- the wrapper refusal a run reports when a function returns a bare
// value instead of a process exit, for instance -- COMPLETED and OBSERVED NOTHING. Collapsing
// that into `holds: False` would be the fabricated plausible output: it would report a failing
// witness where the truth is a witness whose answer was never in a readable shape.
type ClaimObservation
  = ObservedVerdict { holds: v2.std.logic.Bool }
  | ObservationUnrepresentable { reason: v2.std.node.Symbol }

// WHAT THE ROSTER EXPECTED OF THIS IDENTITY, which is an input to the projection and never an
// output of it. Held separately from the observation precisely so that "the witness answered
// false" and "the witness was expected to answer false" stay two facts: their conjunction is
// what makes a red held rather than failing, and a single field could not express the pair.
type ClaimExpectation
  = ExpectedToHold
  | ExpectedRed

// ONE ROW PER PLANNED IDENTITY, AND EXECUTION ORDER IS THE ROW'S POSITION -- there is no
// `ordinal` field, and its absence is the point.
//
// An earlier draft carried `ordinal: v2.std.integer.Int` and claimed in this comment that it made a missing row
// visible as a gap and a truncation visible as a short tail. NOTHING IN THE SCHEMA READ IT. That
// is the rung-inflation shape at carrier grain: a field whose asserted property no code performs,
// where the prose is the only place the guarantee exists.
//
// The repair is not to add a contiguity check -- it is to delete the field. Position in a list is
// dense and unique BY CONSTRUCTION, so a duplicated or gapped ordinal has no spelling here rather
// than being caught by a validator. Truncation is still caught, by the footer count against the
// planned population, which is implemented and tested.
type ClaimTerminalRow {
  identity: v2.workflow.floor2_prepared_subject.WitnessIdentity
  expectation: ClaimExpectation
  terminal: ClaimAttemptTerminal
}

// WITHOUT A BINDING THIS IS A LIST OF ROWS, NOT EVIDENCE -- and the header of this module claims
// CANDIDATE-BOUND witnesses.
//
// An earlier draft had no `Ledger` type at all: only rows, reconciliation and publication. A
// ledger produced by run A was then structurally indistinguishable from evidence for run B, and
// the completed claim the obligation names -- "identity W appears exactly once in candidate C's
// terminal population" -- had no C to name. That is not a missing convenience; it is the
// difference between a ledger and evidence, and the artifact's lifecycle is v2's to own.
//
// The binding is a FIELD OF THE LEDGER rather than a sibling artifact, so an unbound ledger is
// unrepresentable rather than merely unusual. Its three identities are content hashes from
// `std.content_hash` `std.content_hash.Fnv1a64Structural` -- the structural family member DESIGN names, not a bare
// `v2.std.text.String`. A `v2.std.text.String` digest admits any text at all and cannot be compared against anything else
// in the corpus that hashes, which is the hollow-carrier shape.
// A LOCAL RUN HAS NO CANDIDATE, AND THAT IS AN EVIDENTIAL FACT RATHER THAN A FORMATTING ONE.
//
// The completed claim this ledger exists to support is "witness W terminated in candidate C". A
// run with no published commit has no C, so its ledger is NOT candidate-bound and must not be
// citable as though it were. Rendering the seed's `"local"` into a commit-shaped field would be
// the state-space conflation trap in its smallest disguise: a consumer asking "is this
// candidate-bound?" would have to string-compare a magic value, and the day someone spells it
// differently the answer silently flips.
//
// So it is an ARM. `GitObjectId` cannot represent "no commit" -- that is the type doing its job
// -- and the absence gets its own case, which makes an unbound ledger unmistakable from the
// shape instead of by convention.
//
// The type is `GitObjectId` and NOT a freshly minted repository-ref type. One exists, it is
// cited to the upstream authority with validating constructors that enforce family, length and
// lowercase syntax, and v2 already binds artifacts to it -- `v2.compiler.self_host`
// `frontier_probe_types` carries `source_commit` and `source_tree` as `GitObjectId` for exactly
// this purpose. Minting a `RepositoryRef` beside it would be the §3 nicknaming violation
// committed inside a change whose subject is single authority.
type RepositorySnapshot
  = PublishedCommit { commit: GitObjectId }
  | UnpublishedWorkingTree

// THE REF AND THE DIGEST ARE TWO FACTS, NOT ONE, so they are two fields. The ref says WHICH
// commit a human opens; the prepared-subject digest says WHAT was actually admitted, and they
// diverge on every run this floor performs, because preparation declines sites. A binding that
// carried only one of them would answer only half of "which candidate is this evidence for".
//
// `repository_snapshot` could not have been an `Fnv1a64Structural` even if we had wanted it to
// be: that carrier is `String where lower_hex_16`, so a 40-hex commit id REFUSES AT
// CONSTRUCTION. The corpus settled this before the question was asked.
// THE ROSTER IDENTITY IS NOT A FIELD, BECAUSE IT IS NOT A FACT A CALLER HOLDS.
//
// An earlier revision carried `roster_identity: Fnv1a64Structural` here, supplied by whoever
// built the binding. That is a stored value which can DISAGREE with the population it names, and
// a binding whose roster identity describes a different roster than the rows is worse than
// carrying none: it is a false claim about which witnesses this evidence covers. It is also a
// fact nothing in the floor computes -- inventing a parameter for a value that does not exist is
// how hollow carriers start.
//
// So it is derived at render time from the identities actually being published, by the same fold
// on both sides. A roster identity that disagrees with its rows has no constructor rather than
// being caught by a check (§5).
type LedgerBinding {
  repository_snapshot: RepositorySnapshot
  prepared_subject: Fnv1a64Structural
}

fn ledger_is_candidate_bound(binding: LedgerBinding) -> Bool {
  match binding.repository_snapshot {
    PublishedCommit { commit: _ } => true
    UnpublishedWorkingTree => false
  }
}

type TerminalLedger {
  binding: LedgerBinding
  rows: List<ClaimTerminalRow>
  publication: LedgerPublication
}

// THE PROJECTION IS LOSSLESS, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT'S WAS NOT.
//
// That draft sent RuntimeErrored, HostToolUnresolved, RouteGap and ObservationUnrepresentable all
// to one `RefusedBeforeVerdict`. The row had just been un-collapsed for exactly this reason -- a
// budget refusal, a runtime error and a witness that answered false have different remedies -- and
// the disposition re-collapsed them one step later, for the consumer that most needs them apart.
//
// It is not a cosmetic loss. The floor already reports these as SEPARATE COUNTERS
// (`host_tool_unresolved`, `route_gap_unenrolled`, `route_gap_held`, `stale_route_gap`,
// `interrupted_before_verdict`), and the obligation requires those counters to be DERIVED from
// the finalized rows so that a counter disagreeing with the ledger has no constructor. A
// projection that has already merged two counters cannot derive either, so the counters would
// have had to be computed independently -- restoring the dual authority the derivation exists to
// dissolve. One arm per terminal shape keeps every counter derivable.

// `Pass` IS A PROJECTION, NOT A STORED FIELD, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE CONSTRUCTION.
//
// There is no `passed: v2.std.logic.Bool` anywhere in the row, so a row cannot disagree with its own
// disposition -- the disagreement has no constructor. The seed's `ClaimOutcome::Pass =>
// outcome.passed += 1` is an INDEPENDENT AUTHORITY over the same fact, and two authorities over
// one fact is the defect this dissolves: the counter is derived from the finalized rows, so a
// counter that disagrees with the ledger is unrepresentable rather than detected by a
// cross-check.
type ClaimDisposition
  = Passed
  | Failed
  | KnownRedHeld
  | KnownRedNowPassing
  | BudgetRefusedBeforeVerdict
  | HostToolUnresolvedBeforeVerdict
  | RouteGapBeforeVerdict
  | RuntimeErroredBeforeVerdict
  | ObservationUnreadableBeforeVerdict

fn claim_disposition(row: ClaimTerminalRow) -> ClaimDisposition {
  match row.terminal {
    BudgetRefused { safety: _ } => BudgetRefusedBeforeVerdict
    RuntimeErrored { message: _ } => RuntimeErroredBeforeVerdict
    HostToolUnresolved { name: _, probed: _ } => HostToolUnresolvedBeforeVerdict
    RouteGap { route: _ } => RouteGapBeforeVerdict
    Returned { observation: o } =>
      match o {
        ObservationUnrepresentable { reason: _ } => ObservationUnreadableBeforeVerdict
        ObservedVerdict { holds: h } =>
          match row.expectation {
            ExpectedToHold => if h { Passed } else { Failed }
            ExpectedRed    => if h { KnownRedNowPassing } else { KnownRedHeld }
          }
      }
  }
}

// COMPLETENESS IS AN IDENTITY JOIN, NEVER A COUNT EQUALITY, AND HERE IS THE CASE THAT PROVES IT.
//
// Suppose one planned identity never reaches the ledger and some other identity is written twice.
// `planned == terminal_rows` holds. `passed + failed + held == planned` holds. Every aggregate
// count in the run summary is IDENTICAL to a correct run. A count-based check is green, and the
// missing witness -- possibly the one the author just added -- is reported as covered.
//
// So the reconciliation below computes THREE INDEPENDENT SETS by joining on identity, and it is a
// RECORD rather than a coproduct on purpose: a coproduct would report the first defect it found
// and hide the second, and this failure mode is exactly the one where two defects appear together
// and cancel in the totals. Completeness is all three being empty, which the swap above fails in
// two places at once while no count moves.
type LedgerReconciliation {
  planned_without_terminal: List<v2.workflow.floor2_prepared_subject.WitnessIdentity>
  terminal_without_planned: List<v2.workflow.floor2_prepared_subject.WitnessIdentity>
  terminal_duplicated: List<v2.workflow.floor2_prepared_subject.WitnessIdentity>
}

fn ledger_is_complete(r: LedgerReconciliation) -> v2.std.logic.Bool {
  r.planned_without_terminal.length() == 0
    && r.terminal_without_planned.length() == 0
    && r.terminal_duplicated.length() == 0
}

// A ROUTED IDENTITY WITH NO TERMINAL ROW IS INCOMPLETE, NEVER GREEN, and a terminal row for an
// identity nobody planned is FOREIGN EXECUTION and is refused. The two are separate arms because
// their remedies differ: the first says the fold lost a claim, the second says something executed
// that the roster never admitted, and collapsing them would let a lost claim be explained away as
// a stray one.

// PUBLICATION IS PART OF THE EVIDENCE, SO ITS FAILURE MUST BE PART OF THE VERDICT.
//
// A ledger is finalized only after the fold completes: the footer carries the row count and a
// digest over the rows, and the file is published atomically under its final name. A run killed
// before finalization therefore leaves either no ledger or one that is VISIBLY partial -- it never
// leaves a complete-looking file that is short.
//
// `LedgerPartial` is not a degraded success. Witnesses all passing plus a failed ledger write is
// EVIDENCE INCOMPLETE, and evidence incomplete is not green: otherwise the instrumentation is
// optional exactly when it fails, which is the failure arm that widens instead of refusing.
type LedgerPublication
  = LedgerFinalized { row_count: v2.std.integer.Int, digest: v2.std.text.String }
  | LedgerPartial { reason: v2.std.node.Symbol }

// GREEN ADMISSION IS A TYPED OUTCOME, NOT A v2.std.logic.Bool, because a v2.std.logic.Bool cannot say WHY evidence was
// refused and the three reasons have three different remedies: the fold lost rows, something
// executed that was never planned, or the run died before the ledger was finalized. Returning
// `False` for all three would be the state-space conflation this schema exists to remove -- one
// silence standing for several states -- committed by the very function that judges completeness.
//
// THE FOOTER'S COUNT IS CHECKED AGAINST THE PLANNED POPULATION, not against the rows the reader
// happened to parse. Comparing the footer to the rows read would be an identity: a truncation that
// removed rows AND rewrote the footer to match would agree with itself. The planned population is
// the independent referent, and it is the one the caller already holds.
type LedgerGreenAdmission
  = EvidenceComplete
  | EvidenceUnfinalized { reason: v2.std.node.Symbol }
  | EvidenceReconciliationFailed { reconciliation: LedgerReconciliation }
  | EvidenceFooterCountDisagrees { footer_rows: v2.std.integer.Int, planned_rows: v2.std.integer.Int }

fn ledger_green_admission(
  ledger: TerminalLedger,
  reconciliation: LedgerReconciliation,
  planned: List<v2.workflow.floor2_prepared_subject.WitnessIdentity>,
) -> LedgerGreenAdmission {
  match ledger.publication {
    LedgerPartial { reason: r } => EvidenceUnfinalized { reason: r }
    LedgerFinalized { row_count: n, digest: _ } =>
      if ledger_is_complete(r: reconciliation) {
        if n == planned.length() {
          EvidenceComplete
        } else {
          EvidenceFooterCountDisagrees { footer_rows: n, planned_rows: planned.length() }
        }
      } else {
        EvidenceReconciliationFailed { reconciliation: reconciliation }
      }
  }
}
